WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.000
Hey everyone, welcome back to another episode of the Grand Prixquel Podcast where we discuss all things feeder series.

00:06.000 --> 00:14.000
On this episode, we're going to be doing a recap of the F2 and F3 rounds at the Hungaroring, as well as Italian F4 at Mugello.

00:14.000 --> 00:18.000
Your hosts today are myself Evan, Alex and Nova.

00:18.000 --> 00:30.000
So I guess we can get straight into F2 then, and straight into qualifying.

00:30.000 --> 00:37.000
We had a top 10 of Maini, Camara, Leon, Durksen, Inthraphuvasak, Van Hoepen, Tsolov, Beganovic, Mini, Montoya.

00:37.000 --> 00:41.000
Top 10 of all time I think.

00:41.000 --> 00:47.000
Agreed. It's really unfortunate we didn't get to see Dunne in the top 10.

00:47.000 --> 00:55.000
He ended up P17, he had a suspension failure, as he said on his Instagram after the session.

00:55.000 --> 01:04.000
But I think just the top 3 alone, Maini, Camara, Leon, it's the concept to say the least.

01:04.000 --> 01:12.000
It's the concept. We know that the Invicta is capable of that, and we've seen Leon set a pole before.

01:13.000 --> 01:17.000
The Maini thing definitely took me off guard.

01:17.000 --> 01:24.000
We're at the same thing that I said yesterday with Inthraphuvasak also being P5.

01:24.000 --> 01:29.000
How's the ART just really going off this year?

01:29.000 --> 01:35.000
And it's obviously the year where there's like drivers you wouldn't really expect.

01:35.000 --> 01:38.000
I'm sorry, but you didn't really care for it.

01:38.000 --> 01:47.000
Last year we had drivers and the years before we had drivers that were relevant and had shots at F1,

01:47.000 --> 01:52.000
but now we're stuck in Maini and Inthraphuvasak. Come on.

01:52.000 --> 02:01.000
And it's also really interesting to see people like Tsolov and Mini lower down the order.

02:02.000 --> 02:05.000
I mean, Campos had a weird strategy.

02:05.000 --> 02:16.000
I've seen some people on Twitter discuss it, that they thought that if Tsolov was on the strategy they put Leon on,

02:16.000 --> 02:19.000
he would probably be P3, but we never know.

02:19.000 --> 02:30.000
I think he could have done more probably, but also Hungary is a track that just leads to a bit of chaos every time.

02:30.000 --> 02:36.000
And we managed to get Montoya on sprint pole again. Oh god.

02:36.000 --> 02:43.000
Montoya, Montoya. I guess, you know, perfect segue over to the sprint race then where we've got a bit more things.

02:43.000 --> 02:50.000
Montoya initially, I say initially, Montoya initially actually did listen to our advice,

02:50.000 --> 02:53.000
which means he actually listens to the podcast then, allegedly.

02:53.000 --> 02:58.000
He pressed the throttle and he actually got away pretty well over Mini.

02:58.000 --> 03:02.000
I think in the first couple of laps, he already opened up at least an over one second gap.

03:02.000 --> 03:04.000
It was like a one point something second gap.

03:04.000 --> 03:09.000
So it's lovely, great to see from him, obviously.

03:09.000 --> 03:14.000
But then when we had DRS and then the gap closed a bit, but I think he pressed the throttle.

03:14.000 --> 03:17.000
So our episode title came true.

03:17.000 --> 03:20.000
He actually did learn and he learned to press the throttle.

03:20.000 --> 03:25.000
JPM probably very happy that he probably did that for once.

03:25.000 --> 03:32.000
Dunne as well. Dunne's entire race was just, I think his suspension was still pretty messed up.

03:32.000 --> 03:36.000
And I think Rodin just didn't, I guess, didn't fix it properly.

03:36.000 --> 03:40.000
Maybe they couldn't, something along those lines, but he had a bad race.

03:40.000 --> 03:43.000
Clearly did not finish in the top eight because I don't have the results here of where he finished.

03:43.000 --> 03:51.000
But yeah, bad time for him still after everything happened qualifying for him.

03:52.000 --> 04:02.000
If Montoya listened to us and we got him to press the throttle, maybe it's time we start giving more advice.

04:02.000 --> 04:06.000
My next advice would be stop crashing.

04:06.000 --> 04:11.000
We all just got to be a bit more down to earth and more safe.

04:11.000 --> 04:13.000
Don't try any huge moves.

04:13.000 --> 04:25.000
The way Tsolov did on Montoya, like, you know, it was surely moved down the inside where it's like, yeah, that's a Red Bull move.

04:25.000 --> 04:31.000
But it was a move that was not necessarily needed for the championship.

04:31.000 --> 04:42.000
Let's just say that because–did we go through the finished…did we go through the final race results?

04:42.000 --> 04:45.000
Well, yeah, let's go through the top eight then.

04:45.000 --> 04:47.000
So we had Mini winning the race.

04:47.000 --> 04:49.000
Beganovic, Van Hoepen on the podium.

04:49.000 --> 04:55.000
Camara, Maini, Inthraphuvasak, Durksen and Stenshorne to round off the top eight.

04:55.000 --> 05:02.000
Because Tsolov just pretty much absolutely threw away a podium there.

05:02.000 --> 05:07.000
Due to that, obviously Camara also gained positions for free.

05:07.000 --> 05:17.000
Mini had more points for free that, you know, if he would have been on a podium, like Mini would have gained like two or three points in him or something like that.

05:17.000 --> 05:23.000
But now that he's off the podium, it was like 10 points gained.

05:23.000 --> 05:27.000
You know, it's always go for the gap, go for the win, you know, I get it.

05:27.000 --> 05:34.000
But simultaneously, I think in this case, going for this gap.

05:34.000 --> 05:46.000
And if you know it's Montoya and you know Montoya is maybe not the one driver that always is the best at also, you know, reacting to what you're going to put on the track.

05:46.000 --> 05:55.000
Maybe it's time to be a bit more safe, be a bit more in touch with your safety side.

05:55.000 --> 06:03.000
Look, safety is great and all, but it also doesn't give us absolutely interesting moves that we see.

06:03.000 --> 06:16.000
Like again, like we mentioned with the Montoya-Tsolov incident, which he did actually, once we got into the feature race, what I recall, he actually did get that move done, and he got it done properly and safely this time.

06:16.000 --> 06:24.000
But, you know, if we were all that safe, then we wouldn't have even gotten that move at all this entire weekend.

06:24.000 --> 06:36.000
The thing is just, I actually pretty much, I think Tsolov is a good driver and I think, interesting because like, you know, he's in conversation to be potentially up for an F1 seat.

06:36.000 --> 06:47.000
But this kind of thing just ruins your race and it's the kind of thing that can also, in long term, not just cost you the championship, but also cost you the whole ass F1 seat.

06:47.000 --> 06:53.000
And I'm just saying it would be pretty sh*t if that happens for him and I'd be really disappointed.

06:54.000 --> 07:03.000
Predicting all the F1 data accounts, reposting that incident and that move and that crash and being like, ‘he's so bad, he doesn't deserve an F1 seat, blah, blah, blah.’

07:03.000 --> 07:06.000
That's how the cookie crumbles with those accounts.

07:06.000 --> 07:10.000
Anywho, we're not an account about, we're not a podcast about F1 accounts.

07:10.000 --> 07:12.000
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

07:12.000 --> 07:14.000
No, that's what I mentioned though, anyway.

07:14.000 --> 07:16.000
Moving on, I guess.

07:16.000 --> 07:18.000
Feature race.

07:18.000 --> 07:20.000
Again, we'll go through top 10.

07:20.000 --> 07:28.000
That was Leon, Maini, Camara, Van Hoepen, Inthraphuvasak, Beganovic, Tsolov, Durksen, Mini, and Bennett.

07:28.000 --> 07:31.000
And I'm pretty sure that is corrected.

07:31.000 --> 07:39.000
Also, just to mention, Tsolov got a five-place grid drop for the incident with Montoya in the sprint race.

07:39.000 --> 07:46.000
So he started five places below where he actually qualified and is what was meant to start.

07:46.000 --> 07:51.000
On the bright side, for every time that I complain about Tsolov not doing a move right,

07:51.000 --> 07:57.000
Camara also got dog-armed by Leon and Maini.

08:01.000 --> 08:12.000
You know, usually I would say, hey, if there's a great driver, you know it happens sometimes that you are unlucky

08:12.000 --> 08:16.000
and someone maybe crashes you, you don't have the pace.

08:16.000 --> 08:23.000
But if you're Rafael Camara, and like, again, I'm sorry, but if you're in view for an F1 seat,

08:23.000 --> 08:33.000
and you're getting, I mean, I don't want to get bad rep about him again, but I swear he had a fairly slow start

08:33.000 --> 08:36.000
in comparison to Maini and Leon who got away.

08:36.000 --> 08:40.000
At least it looked to me like they got away pretty well.

08:40.000 --> 08:47.000
And that's just the kind of thing that you don't want to happen because at this point it's getting embarrassing.

08:47.000 --> 08:52.000
You're in F2, you really need to make sure that does not happen.

08:52.000 --> 09:01.000
Because again, for him, that just caused a lot of points that he could have on Tsolov who had a really sh*tty weekend.

09:02.000 --> 09:10.000
Speaking of bad weekends for people, not Bilinski, Goethe, or Stenshorne's weekend overall.

09:10.000 --> 09:14.000
I know we did say Stenshorne finished in the top eight, but...

09:17.000 --> 09:23.000
That is also a fair point. It is never Goethe's weekend, but it was particularly not his weekend this week.

09:23.000 --> 09:30.000
But Bilinski had a bad start, he just couldn't get away on the start, and then something happened to him.

09:30.000 --> 09:34.000
I think he went in the pit lane, I think he did get going, and then...

09:34.000 --> 09:43.000
He did. He missed the formation lap, but then he had to start from the pit lane, but not his weekend.

09:43.000 --> 09:49.000
Bad weekend for those three, but expected weekend actually for Goethe, technically speaking.

09:49.000 --> 09:53.000
I think overall, very little action happened.

09:53.000 --> 10:00.000
Tsolov got a handful of moves done, but it's against backmarkers.

10:00.000 --> 10:05.000
So it's like, wow, he got past Nicolas Varrone.

10:05.000 --> 10:11.000
It's just like, I like him, and also the Van Amersfoort, that's like nothing. Doesn't mean anything.

10:11.000 --> 10:19.000
Other than that, also, honourable mention to Dunne, locking up and going sideways in the pit lane for a little bit.

10:19.000 --> 10:27.000
That was like seeing your long-lost lover, and I was like, that's prime Dunne.

10:27.000 --> 10:30.000
Please come back home, the kids miss you.

10:31.000 --> 10:41.000
I was getting flashback to last year when this would have been him straight up rear-ending Martins,

10:41.000 --> 10:49.000
and it's like, you know, we did not appreciate how good we had it when we had actually drivers on the grid.

10:49.000 --> 10:58.000
I'm not saying everyone is bad, but the level of talent has definitely gone lower,

10:58.000 --> 11:06.000
and there's less drivers who we see with any kind of connection or chances in F1, and that's really sad.

11:08.000 --> 11:20.000
From this weekend, I'd also like to mention that despite Bilinski having the most horrid weekend of his life,

11:20.000 --> 11:28.000
Beganovic, for some reason, has had a pretty good one for once, yeah.

11:28.000 --> 11:40.000
I mean, the podium in the sprint, and then actual points in the feature, and his car didn't stall or die on the first lap or anything.

11:40.000 --> 11:44.000
Oh my gosh, what a miracle.

11:44.000 --> 11:54.000
But I think it's fair to say that it was a pretty uneventful weekend in F2 at least,

11:54.000 --> 12:02.000
and for the driver standings, I think nothing changed, but the gaps are getting closer,

12:02.000 --> 12:07.000
and I think we're in for a really interesting end of season.

12:07.000 --> 12:17.000
I'm incredibly hyped for Monza, because that's always one where I say a lot of important things happen,

12:17.000 --> 12:22.000
but we're still with Tsolov leading the championship, but he's only 20 points in front of Mini,

12:22.000 --> 12:32.000
and 20 points sounds like a lot, but I swear to God, in F2 it is not, and he's also just 22 points ahead of Camara,

12:32.000 --> 12:40.000
like Tsolov is 22 points ahead of Camara, Mini is 2 points ahead of Camara in P3.

12:40.000 --> 12:50.000
Dunne is with 108 points quite a bit down on them, like almost 40 on Camara.

12:50.000 --> 12:59.000
You know, he needs to lock in to get higher, it's looking worse and worse.

12:59.000 --> 13:19.000
And even with a whole ass feature win, Noel Leon is in P5 with 94 points, still 14 points behind Dunne.

13:19.000 --> 13:25.000
I think we'll see some things to still mix up, but obviously the front was the most interesting.

13:25.000 --> 13:36.000
I think team's championship actually kind of changed, because Campos is still leading quite a bit with 261 points.

13:36.000 --> 13:45.000
We had it last week where I said that the top 2-4 were in, was a window of 10 points or something.

13:45.000 --> 13:53.000
Obviously that changed, with Invicta rising from P4 to P2 with 187 points now,

13:53.000 --> 14:05.000
MP lost the position and is now on 176 points, and Rodin is in P4 with 167 points.

14:05.000 --> 14:13.000
I'm not sure if we had ART in the top 5 last week, but they are actually getting fairly close,

14:13.000 --> 14:24.000
and if we keep these results up, they could even get into the top 4, maybe even top 3 of the championship,

14:24.000 --> 14:33.000
because now they're in P5 with 147 points, roughly 30 behind MP in third.

14:33.000 --> 14:40.000
And again, it's for both drivers, and we don't know, we don't know.

14:40.000 --> 14:54.000
I think it's time we go over to F3 and consider whatever that qualifying result is, because I have in front of me, and I have so many questions.

14:54.000 --> 15:04.000
Yeah, I woke up to these results as well, so P4 is certainly interesting to me. Let's go through the top 12.

15:04.000 --> 15:15.000
So for F3 quali, our top 12 are Taponen, great sight to see, I think MP actually locked in this time around,

15:15.000 --> 15:33.000
Slater, P2, Colnaghi, P3, Lacorte in P4, Badoer, P5, Ugochukwu, Nael, Rivera, Nakamura, Xie, Gladysz, and Le.

15:33.000 --> 15:44.000
Here's the thing, once again, Ugochukwu going into the weekend as a championship leader, you do not want to be caught out by guys like Lacorte and Badoer,

15:44.000 --> 15:53.000
like that's not what you want to happen. Like Badoer, he's been pretty fine this whole season,

15:53.000 --> 16:05.000
and Rodin also seems to be kind of fine better than last year at least, but it's still, he didn't want it to happen.

16:05.000 --> 16:14.000
Like I think he had not a good weekend, honestly all things considered. Taponen, as you say, it was really good to see him.

16:14.000 --> 16:25.000
MP still whack with Giusti in, I think like he was in like P14, you know, around that area somewhere.

16:25.000 --> 16:34.000
It was really disappointing to see him not, but I swear he hinted that there were problems.

16:34.000 --> 16:44.000
But other than that, MP having Taponen and Colnaghi in top three was really the best quali result probably.

16:44.000 --> 16:57.000
But also kind of sad because Taponen, Colnaghi, and Giusti are probably the lineup that, I've seen a lot of people say it pre-season, and I agree,

16:57.000 --> 17:06.000
it's like the lineup with, even if we have other teams where like Slater I would say has a higher overall best what he can do,

17:06.000 --> 17:15.000
it's the lineup with the overall, you know, best combined drivers because every other lineup has like at least one driver,

17:16.000 --> 17:25.000
they're not as strong, they're not weak. And even if you say like MP is kind of not all three generational talents,

17:25.000 --> 17:32.000
which is totally fine, it's still three really talents who are here because of their driving, not just because of money.

17:32.000 --> 17:36.000
Technically everyone's here for money, but you get what I mean.

17:36.000 --> 17:46.000
Yeah, yeah, I do get what you mean. MP does have the overall, I wouldn't, I'm not going to say best or strongest, but combined, yeah,

17:46.000 --> 17:51.000
their results should show more than what we have right now.

17:51.000 --> 18:00.000
Yeah, like if we assign everyone a number based on how we rate them, theirs would be the highest in team thing.

18:00.000 --> 18:15.000
Anyways, sprint. Sprint started with a lap one collision between I think it was Gladysz and was it Xie? because I think Le got away?

18:15.000 --> 18:21.000
Yeah, he got away, but it was Xie-Gladysz. Was Nakamura involved?

18:21.000 --> 18:31.000
Okay, so this is the thing is that the original incident that was like under investigation, it said Gladysz and Xie,

18:31.000 --> 18:36.000
but Nakamura was the one who was crashed off.

18:36.000 --> 18:47.000
So when Gladys got out of his car all mad, the car next to him wasn't the DAMS, it was the Hitech of Nakamura.

18:48.000 --> 18:58.000
And it's really hard to kind of, I suppose, explain what happened because there were like a thousand, not a thousand, but like obviously it was the midfield area.

18:58.000 --> 19:03.000
So there was like at least seven cars swamping that one corner.

19:03.000 --> 19:13.000
And it's just quite hard to see what happened. But I think yeah, I think it was just a lot of domino effect.

19:13.000 --> 19:18.000
It seemed that Gladysz was just a passenger for the ride quite literally.

19:18.000 --> 19:22.000
I don't think he could have done avoiding action.

19:22.000 --> 19:30.000
Another really sad one because I was actually excited because Gladysz and Le both being in ART.

19:30.000 --> 19:41.000
I'm a bit more fine with if they do well in F3 like the team because there's, sorry, I'm sorry.

19:41.000 --> 19:51.000
I'm a bit more fine with the ART team doing well in F3 because that's drivers who I think also have like higher potential level.

19:51.000 --> 19:57.000
And I was hoping to see a double podium at least, but was Gladysz, you know, crash.

19:57.000 --> 20:01.000
I was a bit sad for him because he also seemed really pissed.

20:01.000 --> 20:04.000
It was really a weekend to forget for him.

20:04.000 --> 20:20.000
But who was able to profit from the situation was once again, Theophile Nael, who won the, did we go through how the race ended?

20:21.000 --> 20:24.000
I don't think so. So it's time.

20:24.000 --> 20:36.000
So it was won by Theophile Nael on the podium, joined by Kanato Le and Rivera, whose first name I sadly forgot.

20:36.000 --> 20:41.000
I'm sorry, Ernesto Rivera. Good guy, good guy.

20:41.000 --> 20:49.000
He's really, you know, getting the points also for Campos who's once again locked in team.

20:49.000 --> 21:00.000
The rest of the points go to Colnaghi, Badoer, Taponen, Lacorte and Wharton, which certainly is a combination of drivers.

21:00.000 --> 21:04.000
The bottom of that top eight is certainly something.

21:04.000 --> 21:15.000
The thing is, what you might have noticed now is that there's two drivers missing here that you would usually expect to see the points, our championship leaders, Slater and Ugochukwu.

21:15.000 --> 21:24.000
Slater, starting from P11, had, you know, a sh*t time anyways, because what do you do starting from P11?

21:24.000 --> 21:32.000
Not a lot, especially on the Hungaroring, which is famously called Monaco without walls, there’s

21:32.000 --> 21:35.000
Even here, we saw DRS trains.

21:35.000 --> 21:39.000
It was pretty mild.

21:39.000 --> 21:41.000
It's not a good track for overtaking.

21:41.000 --> 21:52.000
Anyways, this was not helped by the fact that Ugochukwu, who did go into a good position to start, got a radio call being like, hey, take care of your tires.

21:52.000 --> 21:55.000
No, like there's a bit of tire degradation.

21:55.000 --> 21:58.000
You're like, be careful about it.

21:58.000 --> 22:04.000
And because, you know, we're all grown guys, we know how to do this.

22:04.000 --> 22:07.000
He answered with, you know, I know what I'm doing.

22:07.000 --> 22:10.000
Let me do this.

22:10.000 --> 22:16.000
Turns out he didn't know what he was doing because later on his tires were dead and he was just dropping down like dead weight.

22:16.000 --> 22:32.000
But at the very least for himself, he was able to kind of keep Slater from pushing further and kind of boxed him in, in a way, which was, again, able because of that DRS train.

22:32.000 --> 22:42.000
But it was just one of those moves where I again thought as at-the-time championship leader, do you want to do that?

22:42.000 --> 22:45.000
Like, do you want to win your championship?

22:45.000 --> 22:47.000
And I've said it.

22:47.000 --> 23:01.000
Be safe, like don't crash at your opponent, but also maybe don't over push your tires when his teammate who started one place ahead of him won the race.

23:01.000 --> 23:03.000
He didn't get points.

23:03.000 --> 23:09.000
There is something to consider here.

23:09.000 --> 23:19.000
Yeah, I think, I think part of it for Ugochukwu is that he hasn't really been-

23:19.000 --> 23:30.000
I suppose you could say this far into a title fight on like one of the like, main seasons, if that makes sense.

23:30.000 --> 23:47.000
What I remember is that's one of the biggest criticism points of him because while he's quick, it just feels like he can't put it together over a whole season, especially if, you know, season is longer.

23:47.000 --> 23:51.000
And that's just something you don't want to happen.

23:52.000 --> 24:00.000
Like, he did win Euro-4, but thankfully Euro-4 has like three weekends.

24:00.000 --> 24:03.000
He won Macau, Macau.

24:03.000 --> 24:05.000
Again, it's one weekend.

24:05.000 --> 24:11.000
It's like, if it's a short season like quick race, he can do well.

24:11.000 --> 24:14.000
Or he won FROT.

24:14.000 --> 24:19.000
Again, it was pretty short if I remember correctly.

24:19.000 --> 24:23.000
I think it was like five rounds or something.

24:23.000 --> 24:24.000
Either way.

24:24.000 --> 24:25.000
Yeah.

24:25.000 --> 24:42.000
The thing is, he can be quick, but when he starts into a weekend and the qualifying doesn't go his way and like first race already also doesn't go his way, he can't, he doesn't seem to be able to do the whole thing for [???]

24:42.000 --> 24:51.000
And it's really sad because I think in singular race, he's always, he always has the pace and he always has the ability.

24:51.000 --> 24:58.000
But I'm just continuously, you know, kind of like, what are we doing here?

24:58.000 --> 25:06.000
He could still be leading his championship if he didn't do these mistakes, you know?

25:06.000 --> 25:07.000
Yeah.

25:07.000 --> 25:18.000
I definitely feel like a lot of Ugochukwu's, like his season so far, a lot of it can just be attributed to him.

25:18.000 --> 25:29.000
It's a lot of literally like, I think he just needs to find a way to properly lock it in over a slightly longer period of time.

25:29.000 --> 25:33.000
Because if not, then we wouldn't be seeing, I mean, all this.

25:33.000 --> 25:36.000
But he had it at the beginning.

25:36.000 --> 25:45.000
I mean, he definitely has the talent and I think he's definitely driving a lot of people like because they know that he should be able to do it.

25:45.000 --> 25:48.000
He just has to do, you know, something.

25:48.000 --> 26:03.000
And either ways, I think it's time to go for the feature race because as I've spoiled, Slater took the championship lead by winning the race and being his first feature race win.

26:03.000 --> 26:06.000
It took a lot longer than we expected.

26:06.000 --> 26:15.000
Yeah, especially for someone who's in a Trident and also in general, he's Freddie Slater.

26:15.000 --> 26:18.000
But I'll argue like the Trident thing.

26:18.000 --> 26:29.000
I'll argue that even though we're in the Campos run this season, like I know a lot of people being like, oh my God, Trident and Invicta are the fastest and Invicta still over single lap.

26:29.000 --> 26:30.000
Yes.

26:30.000 --> 26:44.000
But I think that honestly, the Campos this season is better than the Trident in F3 because we see it that the main thing, the main problem that Ugochukwu and Nael have

26:44.000 --> 26:47.000
is that they are, in my opinion, too crash-prone.

26:47.000 --> 26:50.000
I'm like, there's too much going on there.

26:50.000 --> 26:54.000
Rivera, who's, unless I'm absolutely talking bullsh*t.

26:54.000 --> 26:58.000
Wait, let me check before I say something completely wrong.

26:58.000 --> 27:03.000
And we can put that down on the list of Googling.

27:03.000 --> 27:13.000
Yeah, Rivera, who even missed the first round, he's like in the third Campos and you'd think he's like the weakest Campos.

27:13.000 --> 27:19.000
He is still, I mean, you know, Trident drivers, we can discuss that.

27:19.000 --> 27:37.000
But I think he has had quite a stronger season than like, compare him to Stromsted, who's also in the Trident, but not doing what Rivera is doing, even though he has more experience.

27:37.000 --> 27:44.000
Or he's at least not able to do what Rivera, maybe Rivera is just an absolutely great driver and we all underrated him so far.

27:44.000 --> 27:46.000
Who knows?

27:46.000 --> 28:03.000
Either way, the first, like, not half, but like, not even lap, but like in the start of the race was pretty thing because a lot of drivers struggled to get away and like it was kind of wack.

28:03.000 --> 28:10.000
But then, you know, Taponen led, but it's an MP, they don't have that pace.

28:10.000 --> 28:25.000
A bit of fighting, a bit of, you know, touching with Slater and then there was, I think it was a safety car, has to be, or at least a virtual safety car.

28:25.000 --> 28:28.000
Because Taponen had a puncture.

28:28.000 --> 28:34.000
It was a really weird situation in which the team was like, hey, you have a puncture, come in, and he comes in.

28:34.000 --> 28:38.000
The problem is everyone followed.

28:38.000 --> 28:53.000
It was actually a thing where they were, it took way too long to discuss that because it had to all be under investigation because regularly you're not allowed to enter the pits just for sh*ts and giggles.

28:53.000 --> 29:09.000
But if I remember correctly, I, in that moment thought, okay, you know, maybe something happened on the main straight that everyone was told, you know, go through the pits to avoid what happened on the main straight.

29:09.000 --> 29:22.000
You know, just like it often happens that when there's a safety car, they can tell them, ‘hey, take another route so you avoid the things going on there.’

29:22.000 --> 29:28.000
But in this case, it was just everyone kind of assuming Taponen knows where to go.

29:28.000 --> 29:31.000
Tapponen just wanted to do a pit stop.

29:31.000 --> 29:45.000
I mean, I guess it's, I don't know if it's better or worse for him because on one hand side, he had to lead a few guys through because he couldn't just rejoin right when he was finished.

29:45.000 --> 29:55.000
But on the other hand, regularly, if everyone would have done, would have gone over the main straight, he would have ended really dead last.

29:55.000 --> 30:02.000
So I guess he was kind of lucky in that regard that even if he got that puncture, everyone else was stupid.

30:02.000 --> 30:05.000
So he was still in the point positions later on.

30:05.000 --> 30:07.000
Oh, good for him.

30:07.000 --> 30:16.000
Yeah, it was like 26 cars, like literally the whole grid just followed him through and decided ‘yep, that must be it.

30:16.000 --> 30:19.000
Not been told anything, but that must be it.’

30:19.000 --> 30:33.000
I genuinely believe that if just like three or four guys would have done this, they would have penalized them.

30:33.000 --> 30:48.000
I genuinely believe that because the problem was that if 26 cars do it, the only cars that wouldn't have been investigated were Tapponen and the guys that had already DNF'd.

30:48.000 --> 30:52.000
And if, you know, I don't know what the regular penalty for that would be.

30:52.000 --> 31:01.000
But if we say it's like, I mean, in this case, they take it as like, my god, you know, it just happened because it happens to the best of us.

31:01.000 --> 31:03.000
That is not a good explanation, my opinion.

31:03.000 --> 31:07.000
But let's say it was a five, it would have been a five second penalty.

31:07.000 --> 31:17.000
Considering how close they were finished, it would not have any impact, except for the fact that Tapponen probably would have done and even though, he wouldn’t have won.

31:17.000 --> 31:25.000
But he at least would have been quite a few points further up and it would have just been bullsh*t.

31:25.000 --> 31:28.000
And all the teams would have complained and all the drivers would have complained.

31:28.000 --> 31:31.000
Like, let's just let them do this.

31:31.000 --> 31:35.000
I think it's just one of those things where you can't fight that.

31:35.000 --> 31:40.000
I think the stewards probably just wanted to go home.

31:40.000 --> 31:43.000
Like it took so long for that to come out.

31:43.000 --> 31:45.000
They wanted to go home.

31:45.000 --> 31:47.000
Yeah.

31:47.000 --> 31:51.000
Either ways, it was a very typical F3 race after that.

31:51.000 --> 32:00.000
You know, the AIX doing just whatever, a lot of just whatever happening going on.

32:00.000 --> 32:07.000
Slater won as said, Ugochukwu could not get up to him and, you know, really fight for the win.

32:07.000 --> 32:12.000
It did seem to me again that he just was not.

32:12.000 --> 32:19.000
I mean again, Hungaroring, it's not easy to overtake anyways, but I think that could have been done more.

32:19.000 --> 32:23.000
But that can also always be done more.

32:23.000 --> 32:24.000
Yeah.

32:24.000 --> 32:26.000
I think that's round about it.

32:26.000 --> 32:31.000
I think there's a lot more important things to say about F3, except for the-

32:31.000 --> 32:37.000
I do want to very quickly mention that at some point during the race,

32:37.000 --> 32:47.000
we had Jose Garfias, who usually runs about P20, P24-ish, somehow managed to get his way up into P11,

32:47.000 --> 32:51.000
which meant we were very close to triple Prema points.

32:51.000 --> 32:57.000
I sometimes forget he exists.

32:57.000 --> 33:00.000
Yeah, me too.

33:00.000 --> 33:04.000
I'm going to be honest, it's a pretty weak F3 grid also.

33:04.000 --> 33:07.000
And sometimes I just look at it and I'm like.

33:07.000 --> 33:17.000
Especially down below where it's like drivers who don't come from like FREC or Eurocup 3 or like any regular series.

33:17.000 --> 33:20.000
It's like, who are you? What are you doing here?

33:20.000 --> 33:22.000
I'm sorry. Who are you?

33:22.000 --> 33:32.000
And even with FRECA drivers, like you got to have done like the whole season and maybe not in like the last place.

33:32.000 --> 33:34.000
Like, that would help, please.

33:34.000 --> 33:36.000
Anyways.

33:36.000 --> 33:38.000
Championship Standings.

33:38.000 --> 33:44.000
Slater is leading now with 130 points to Ugochukwu's 122 points.

33:44.000 --> 33:58.000
They're a pretty far set off, but nothing is set yet with Nael, who's in P3 with 75 points and technically could still win it all.

33:58.000 --> 34:01.000
Who knows? Who knows? I'm afraid of Madrid.

34:01.000 --> 34:03.000
I've said it a dozen times, I'm afraid.

34:03.000 --> 34:11.000
Rivera follows very shortly with 72 points in P4 and in P5, but with 71 points.

34:11.000 --> 34:18.000
So we will 100% see some changes in like at least P3 to P5.

34:18.000 --> 34:23.000
Like there will be changes and I think it'll matter because we will see.

34:24.000 --> 34:48.000
I mean, I'm just saying if Rivera finishes in the top three, which can happen, I think it makes his likelihood of going to F2 instantly a lot higher than right now where I'm still kind of assuming that he will repeat F3 in hopes of like, genuinely winning it.

34:48.000 --> 34:54.000
Because after a season like this, you can only return to F3 to say you want to win it.

34:54.000 --> 35:04.000
But then again, he will know that coming from FREC and so, are drivers with more money and more possibilities also.

35:04.000 --> 35:15.000
And even if he has the connection to Campos and he saves Campos and is like the most experienced driver, I would be cautious if I was him.

35:15.000 --> 35:36.000
Either ways, Campos is in my opinion, at least driver wise, they even if I complain about them, they managed to get more out of the car by also in teams championships being P1 with 269 points to Trident's 196 points.

35:37.000 --> 35:50.000
Majorly because Campos has three drivers regularly on the podium, while at Trident it's pretty much Slater hauling the whole points.

35:51.000 --> 36:01.000
In P3 we have Van Amersfoort racing with 132 points, P4 is Rodin with 121 and P5 ART with 119 points.

36:01.000 --> 36:09.000
So pretty close once again in that 3 to 5 range of teams.

36:09.000 --> 36:19.000
It's a good thing, I think, and it lines up that I think we could also see a drivers champion come from another team in the next few years.

36:21.000 --> 36:26.000
Do we want to go on to our favorite Formula 4 series?

36:27.000 --> 36:29.000
Italian F4.

36:29.000 --> 36:35.000
My favorite Formula 4 series is actually British F4, but we're not going to discuss that today.

36:36.000 --> 36:44.000
No, Italian F4 was at Mugello this time around.

36:46.000 --> 36:57.000
Already starting with Qualis, we had something very interesting happen where in our Quali 1 and our Quali 2 groups, the polls were matched to the thousands.

36:58.000 --> 37:06.000
In Quali 1 it was Weiss who set it, and in Quali 2 it was Cosma who set it.

37:07.000 --> 37:21.000
And I think I remember, okay, so in Quali 1 it was Weiss, Aksoy, Maccagnani, Bondarev, Sammalisto, Hattemer, Bansal, Arn, Borenstein, and Bearman.

37:21.000 --> 37:28.000
And in Quali 2 it was Cosma, Consani, Craigie, Costoya, all the Cs in one row I suppose.

37:29.000 --> 37:35.000
Simek, Al Sulaiti, Repetto, Rechenmacher, Toniolo, and Bernoldi.

37:37.000 --> 37:53.000
So when that pole lap from Quali 2 was first set, obviously there was the immediate confusion of who would take it and why they would take it, and on what grounds the ACI would allow them to take it.

37:53.000 --> 38:03.000
A lot of people said that it should be, obviously it should be Weiss that takes it, because he, I think he set it first?

38:04.000 --> 38:18.000
No, I remember it. It was really funny, because Weiss did it in Quali 1, but Cosma did it in Quali 2, but he did it like seven tenths of the second earlier.

38:19.000 --> 38:37.000
From the start of the session to when they set it, Cosma did it earlier, but it was ruled then that Weiss still got it, because you know, Quali 1 is first on the track and has the worst track conditions.

38:37.000 --> 38:57.000
And I think both ideas are bullsh*t, because it goes by this idea that we kind of know it from other series, where when you're in the same session, you set the same time, whoever set it first because of track evolution and stuff, the guy who did it first did it better.

38:58.000 --> 39:05.000
But I think it's hard to apply that here, but I'm biased and I like Weiss, so I think he should have gotten the pole, and he got it.

39:06.000 --> 39:13.000
Yeah, he did get it in the end, and I think a lot of the, what's it called, like the tiebreakers were in his favor either way.

39:14.000 --> 39:25.000
But I do remember that for a second, ACI did announce that it was Cosma who got in pole, and that just did not make any sense to me. It was the stupidest reasoning for it.

39:25.000 --> 39:27.000
Yeah, they’re all just bullsh*t, whatever.

39:28.000 --> 39:38.000
So combined our top 10 for Quali was Weiss, Cosma, Aksoy, Consani, Maccagnani, Craigie, Bondarev, Costoya, Sammalisto, and Simek.

39:39.000 --> 39:49.000
Once again, once again, I've said it a thousand times before, I think Bondarev isn't even in the conversation really for getting any title anymore.

39:50.000 --> 39:59.000
But I'm saying, I'm still, I've said it so often, I'm almost sorry for him, but he needs to step up.

40:00.000 --> 40:11.000
I mean, I think what I want to add here is that we, over the past few weeks, we've seen quite a lot of testing going on for like FREC and GB3 for like the next season.

40:12.000 --> 40:23.000
And obviously these results at the moment, they really matter because they're what makes drivers decide, hey, do I want to do another season in F4 or do I do the step up?

40:23.000 --> 40:35.000
And it also kind of matters to teams because while, you know, you can just play a team, especially the big teams, they also want to keep their reputation.

40:36.000 --> 40:45.000
And if you were just bumming around and they have the same offer to pay from other drives who are doing better, it's looking bad.

40:46.000 --> 40:54.000
Either way, I'm sorry, I'm sorry for all the Bondarev fans because I know he has a lot and I'm like, I hope he does well.

40:57.000 --> 41:05.000
Don't worry, I came into the season very supportive of Bondarev and now sometimes I kind of go, man, I can't do this anymore.

41:06.000 --> 41:08.000
Anyways, moving on to race one.

41:09.000 --> 41:19.000
For race one, our top ten was Maccagnani, Craigie, Sammalisto, Cosma, Repetto, Hattemer, Aksoy, Costoya, Borenstein, and Ruta.

41:20.000 --> 41:26.000
Just reading off the top ten, I would like to remind everyone that Cosma started on pole.

41:27.000 --> 41:31.000
Unimpressed. We broke Samalisto's winning streak.

41:32.000 --> 41:39.000
Yeah, unfortunately, we did not stop his podium streak, which meant he matched Slater for consecutive podiums here.

41:40.000 --> 41:44.000
Slater, Freddie Slater. What is this grid?

41:45.000 --> 41:48.000
Sammalisto, future F1 driver.

41:49.000 --> 41:57.000
Don't say that! Oh, I was already crashing out about the Italian F4 intro for next year being all Sammalisto.

41:58.000 --> 42:00.000
Don't say that he's a future F1 driver.

42:01.000 --> 42:09.000
On the bright side, Craigie. Craigie had a pretty good race and he's had a really good weekend, actually.

42:10.000 --> 42:18.000
And I've been, I've been happy about it because R-ace did not have the best general time so far.

42:19.000 --> 42:27.000
Like his start to the season was a bit rocky, but it's, I feel like he's doing the opposite of what I criticized with,

42:28.000 --> 42:36.000
with Ugochukwu earlier on in which he is not kind of, you know, losing his pace throughout the season.

42:37.000 --> 42:43.000
But I feel like he's, you know, stepping up. He's getting better with every weekend.

42:44.000 --> 42:52.000
Like I remember his first weekend in Italian F4, it was kind of like, sh*t man, that did not look good.

42:52.000 --> 42:56.000
And I have to check before I say something wrong again.

42:57.000 --> 43:09.000
But yeah, I mean, in the winter series, he may not have started out with like a win, but I feel like you see it in his driving style.

43:10.000 --> 43:16.000
He's getting more, more adventurous. Like he's trying more things. He's daring to do more things.

43:17.000 --> 43:20.000
He's getting that experience he really needs and it's, I think, a great look for him.

43:20.000 --> 43:28.000
Yeah, and it's definitely also considering the fact that he's part of the Mercedes-AMG Junior Team.

43:29.000 --> 43:34.000
I think it reflects just well on him as a driver, but also as a member of that academy.

43:35.000 --> 43:45.000
Yeah, I think we can tell that he's one of the drivers that I've seen mentioned quite a lot of times in connection to testing in FREC already.

43:45.000 --> 43:53.000
And honestly, I'm unsure because, you know, he did not have the best start to the season, but we can also blame R-ace a bit for that.

43:54.000 --> 44:08.000
Either ways, I think if he goes on the way he does right now, he will also improve at the standings and it's probably a good shot for him to also make the way to FREC already.

44:09.000 --> 44:14.000
Especially if you have to consider US Racing domination.

44:15.000 --> 44:20.000
Okay, nuke US racing.

44:21.000 --> 44:28.000
Yeah, we have to be realistic. What does he want to do? Does he want to do R-ace or something in peace?

44:29.000 --> 44:36.000
I heard the rumors that he would do R-ace in FREC or does he want to do US racing just to like gain the title?

44:36.000 --> 44:45.000
But R-ace is the Mercedes Junior Team, so like, let's be realistic.

44:46.000 --> 44:57.000
Either ways, race two. One by my guy, my favorite, I'm really endeared to him, Ary Bansal. Great guy, I think.

44:57.000 --> 45:18.000
Or I'm going to be, you know, shot by Robin if I didn't mention it. Andy Consani would have been P2 if it was not for the fact that Weiss had a horrific weekend in which he actually started on pole.

45:18.000 --> 45:30.000
But Consani kind of like, you know, touched him a little bit, led to his like tire exploding or something. I know it was not exploding, but it's like he DNF’d . That was not good.

45:31.000 --> 45:35.000
You know, I like Weiss . I like Consani. I like both of them. I enjoy both of them.

45:35.000 --> 45:51.000
The question is, was this worth 25 seconds of penalties? Because I don't think so. I have never seen someone get a penalty like that or, you know, something like that.

45:51.000 --> 46:05.000
Yeah, I heard people say it might have been because IF4 goes under that whole repeat offender sort of thing. So if you've done it before, they're going to make it harsher on you the next time around.

46:05.000 --> 46:20.000
But I've never seen that on anyone else in the series. I've had someone tell me that they could imagine that the only reason that they're doing it for Consani is kind of to also protect Consani in the long run.

46:20.000 --> 46:29.000
Because if someone who always ends up P30 doesn't even qualify for, I mean, Consani also doesn't qualify for the fourth race.

46:30.000 --> 46:44.000
But what I'm trying to say is he's a driver that will surely make his way to FREC at some point. He's a driver that we all expect to see definitely for longer in racing.

46:44.000 --> 47:08.000
And they said that–the one who told me this and the one who, you know, mentioned thinking that way–just said, you know, when you have 50 drivers and you have like every single race ends with like at least five DNFs and you have to rate between this guy who's like doing his first ever F4 race who will probably not even go to FREC.

47:08.000 --> 47:18.000
Do you want to give him the big penalty if you're already annoyed by him and you just know they're going to do it anyways again and it won't help?

47:18.000 --> 47:29.000
Or do you look at the guy who's, you know, doing pretty well, actually, but he cannot keep doing these things and, you know, you give him one sh*tty penalty and hope he never does it again?

47:29.000 --> 47:32.000
Yeah, I...

47:32.000 --> 47:35.000
At first I was like, that's ass bullsh*t.

47:35.000 --> 47:41.000
But then I thought if I was a steward, maybe I would actually, you know, get down with it.

47:41.000 --> 47:42.000
I would understand that.

47:42.000 --> 47:49.000
I would maybe actually think the same because right now it's P2 in Italian F4 like whatever.

47:49.000 --> 47:57.000
But if he does sh*t like that, say in the F3 final and he loses the title that way, it's sh*t.

47:57.000 --> 48:00.000
But now he's like, I can never crash another guy.

48:00.000 --> 48:03.000
I get f**king 25 seconds.

48:03.000 --> 48:04.000
Yeah.

48:04.000 --> 48:07.000
Got to be harsh on them kids.

48:07.000 --> 48:08.000
Either ways.

48:08.000 --> 48:09.000
Either ways.

48:09.000 --> 48:14.000
So the final podium was, you know, as I've said, Bansal win.

48:14.000 --> 48:15.000
Great.

48:15.000 --> 48:16.000
Like Bansal.

48:16.000 --> 48:17.000
Great guy.

48:17.000 --> 48:19.000
I hope he does well.

48:19.000 --> 48:25.000
Pretty sure he was also mentioned in connection to Formula Regional already.

48:25.000 --> 48:29.000
In P2 we had Maccagnani, P3 Cosma.

48:29.000 --> 48:34.000
Rest of the points go to Bondarev, Savinkov, Costoya, Bearman.

48:35.000 --> 48:43.000
Bearman was also testing with, I think, what I saw, multiple teams, which is why I'm pointing this out.

48:43.000 --> 48:47.000
Because I saw him test with both Van Amersfoort, I think or MP.

48:47.000 --> 48:49.000
It was an Orange team at least.

48:49.000 --> 48:52.000
And another team, potentially R-ace.

48:52.000 --> 48:58.000
But either ways, a lot of the guys you see on top here are testing at the moment.

48:58.000 --> 49:01.000
And maybe that's something that we should talk about in the future.

49:01.000 --> 49:09.000
Also points to Repetto, Simek, Hattemer, and some other guys who get so little points they don't matter.

49:11.000 --> 49:16.000
I also think it's interesting how well the Trident does on Mugello.

49:16.000 --> 49:25.000
Because usually for IF4 we see maybe like...

49:25.000 --> 49:28.000
I've already forgotten who's in the Trident.

49:28.000 --> 49:31.000
You see maybe Hattemier get into the points.

49:31.000 --> 49:42.000
But at this specific round, and I think it kind of shows in Race 2, a lot of the not lower cars.

49:42.000 --> 49:47.000
But yeah, cars like the Trident were getting pretty consistent points.

49:47.000 --> 49:57.000
Repetto, who's pretty okay, got higher points this time around.

49:57.000 --> 49:59.000
And I thought that was quite interesting.

49:59.000 --> 50:03.000
Yeah, I think it's maybe time to...

50:03.000 --> 50:05.000
No, there's one thing that I forgot.

50:05.000 --> 50:07.000
There's one thing that I forgot.

50:07.000 --> 50:10.000
Oh my god, don't make me forget things.

50:10.000 --> 50:17.000
If we're already talking about PHM racing, we should talk about Emma Felbermayr scoring another top 15 result.

50:17.000 --> 50:20.000
To be precise, P14.

50:20.000 --> 50:26.000
Which is also not like a lot, but it's PHM.

50:26.000 --> 50:32.000
And what I really wanted to point out here is that I think so far this season,

50:32.000 --> 50:38.000
she has been by far, in my opinion, the best performing female driver in this championship.

50:38.000 --> 50:41.000
Because I have multiple reasons for that.

50:41.000 --> 50:45.000
First of all, she is the only female driver to have...

50:45.000 --> 50:49.000
Or no, there's also Rosanne Den Drijver.

50:49.000 --> 50:56.000
But she only did one round, but those two, they have not once not qualified for the last race.

50:56.000 --> 51:01.000
But she did three rounds, and she wasn't...

51:01.000 --> 51:03.000
She didn't...

51:03.000 --> 51:05.000
It's a lot of words. It’s bullsh*t. 

51:05.000 --> 51:10.000
She also has three times finished in the top 15, which again, it helps.

51:10.000 --> 51:15.000
But more importantly, she's been almost exclusively been like...

51:15.000 --> 51:20.000
I think this weekend was once again where she was like the second best PHM driver.

51:20.000 --> 51:24.000
And you know, some of her teammates are questionable.

51:24.000 --> 51:27.000
They have less experience than her and stuff.

51:27.000 --> 51:34.000
But I think it's a good sign that she's, you know, up there and can be somewhat on pace with her teammates.

51:34.000 --> 51:39.000
And you know, can fight further up, even if she has bad luck at some times.

51:39.000 --> 51:45.000
If we compare it to other drivers like Payton Westcott and Emily Cotty, who are both with R-ace GP,

51:45.000 --> 51:52.000
who I definitely expected to do better, because just by technicality, their cars are better.

51:52.000 --> 52:00.000
But both of them, having qualified in certain race fours, have only gotten points once both times in P15,

52:00.000 --> 52:04.000
while Emma Felbermeyr's best finish was P14 twice.

52:04.000 --> 52:11.000
We have other drivers, we have other female drivers who have only done one round like Rosanne Den Drijver 

52:11.000 --> 52:17.000
And Zoe Florescu this weekend, who I'll also talk about later when we talk about race three.

52:17.000 --> 52:26.000
And Rafaela Ferreira, who has the unlucky record of just never really finishing well.

52:27.000 --> 52:33.000
Like that's just sad, I'm sorry. Please, please.

52:34.000 --> 52:38.000
Either way, I think Emma Felbermeyr has shown some great, great things.

52:38.000 --> 52:50.000
And I hope that we see her pop up when it comes to the testing for Formula Regional, because PHM is not, you know, a good team.

52:50.000 --> 53:02.000
There's like, if you look at their team standings, it's regularly just in that 10 to 15 position of where they get their points.

53:02.000 --> 53:10.000
And she's one of the drivers that does help them score points in, you know, the team's championship.

53:10.000 --> 53:16.000
As you've mentioned, who was it?

53:16.000 --> 53:23.000
Repetto is really, I think, outperforming that PHM. I don't know how he does that.

53:23.000 --> 53:32.000
I haven't looked at his stats. I don't know how much experience he has, but I think Felbermeyr has been really good to me.

53:33.000 --> 53:48.000
Yeah, so Race Three started on the safety car, mainly because apparently there's a thunderstorm right before the race was scheduled to start.

53:48.000 --> 53:57.000
And so 30 minutes, it was delayed. But our top 10 on the podium was Consani.

